WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.779
When we think of The Simpsons' holiday episodes,
our thoughts immediately turn to Halloween

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:08.440
due to the wildly successful "Treehouse of
Horror" stories.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.160
However, Springfield's favorite family has
starred in plenty of Christmas adventures.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.119
In fact, here are 15 of them, ranked from
worst to best, according to fans on IMDb.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:23.539
Aired in December 2006, "Kill Gil, Volumes
I &amp; II" finds the Simpsons taking pity on

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:27.259
Springfield's eternally down-on-his-luck businessman,
Gil.

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:29.869
They even invite him to stay at their home
for Christmas.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:34.150
Unfortunately, Marge has a hard time saying
"no," and Gil ends up crashing at their place

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.579
for 11 months.

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:39.530
They're a hilarious 11 months to be sure,
but they push Marge and the family to the

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.530
edge.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:44.540
By the time Marge finally works up the gumption
to kick Gil out, she discovers that he just

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:45.649
left on his own.

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:49.620
However, with a need for closure, the family
drives all the way to Arizona so Marge can

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.960
tell Gil exactly how she feels.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.079
Like most Simpsons episodes, this one is packed
with great lines but there's one that stands

00:00:56.079 --> 00:00:57.079
above the rest.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:00.170
"Why did you let that loser into our home?"

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.290
"I'll tell you why: Christian Charity."

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.290
"Christian Charity?

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:06.290
What does a porn star have to do with this?"

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.070
In the episode "White Christmas Blues," climate
change has made it far too hot for snow to

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.910
fall throughout the vast majority of America
— except for Springfield.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.720
As it turns out, pollution from the town's
nuclear power plant combines with smoke from

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.990
its perpetually burning tire yard to create
freak conditions allowing for snow.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:28.350
So, an influx of tourists dash into Springfield
to experience a traditional white Christmas.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:31.470
The town becomes so overrun that hotels are
turning people away.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.880
In need of holiday money, Marge decides to
take in some boarders, despite Homer's objections.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.080
"Uh-uh, no way.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.630
I'm not sharing my bed with anybody except
you.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.090
And maybe that guy.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.090
Lincoln-style.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.090
But that's it."

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.160
Eventually, the guests become so rude and
demanding that they nearly destroy the Simpsons'

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.090
holiday spirit — but we all know that Marge
would never let that happen.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.140
"The Fight Before Christmas" would go down
as a pretty standard Simpsons holiday episode,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.680
were it not for the final of its four Christmas-themed
segments.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.040
To wrap the episode up, The Simpsons switches
its format from animation to puppetry, and

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.039
the cast turns into full-on felt-and-stuffing
Muppets.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.060
This sudden switch was pretty stunning to
some fans but the show had yet another surprise

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.060
lined up.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.780
After being edited out of a Sesame Street
episode for dressing in a way that some people

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.500
considered too risque for a children's show,
Katy Perry drops by the Simpsons holiday special

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.000
for a live-action celebrity cameo.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.970
Unfortunately, even that wasn't enough to
impress some viewers.

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.450
"Last night, I had a visit from three spirits."

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:36.080
"I wish this show had a visit from three new
writers."

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.080
Penned by O.G.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.900
Simpsons writer and longtime showrunner Al
Jean, "I Won't Be Home for Christmas" finds

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.400
Marge enraged when Homer stays out too late
on Christmas Eve.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:49.460
However, Homer only gets held up because Moe
needs some company to help him through an

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.320
extra-difficult spell of holiday-related loneliness.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.660
This season 26 episode makes plenty of gestures
toward emphasizing the importance of family

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:00.600
and togetherness and all that, but without
any genuine conflict, the story glides through

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.980
your brain without leaving much to remember
it by.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:07.569
Although, in one scene, Homer stumbles across
Gil in a movie theater, which raises the question:

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:10.319
Why is Gil in so many Simpsons holiday episodes?

00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:13.060
We're not even in the top ten yet, and he's
already popped up twice.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:18.530
Once again, Gil manages to find his way into
yet another Simpsons Christmas special.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:23.110
In "'Tis the 30th Season," Marge rescues Gil
from being trampled at a Black Friday sale

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.080
and helps him secure a plush toy for his grandchild.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.501
Later in the episode, Homer and the kids decide
to give Marge a break for the holidays, and

00:03:29.501 --> 00:03:30.840
the family heads to Florida.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.040
The only problem is that Homer books them
a room in a seriously dingy motel.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.290
Plus, the nearby theme park isn't any better
with its shabby Hall of Vice Presidents and

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.130
assortment of bizarre cartoon character mascots.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:46.450
"'Tis the 30th Season" also marks another
instance of the show's writers parodying the

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:51.080
famous "horse head" scene from The Godfather.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:58.620
"I shot my best friend in the face."

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.320
"Simpsons Christmas Stories" stands as the
second anthology episode on this list and

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.300
includes three holiday tales.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:08.599
The middle story is the real standout here
and focuses on Grampa reminiscing to Bart

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:11.630
and Lisa about why he wants to kill Santa
Claus.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:15.630
Without giving too much away, this 2005 episode
features the first and last appearance of

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:21.269
Great Uncle Cyrus Simpson, who lives in Tahiti
with his 15 wives and presumably several Simpsons

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:22.700
cousins and second cousins.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:28.090
It also boasts an amazing flashback sequence
of a young Grampa and Mr. Burns marooned on

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:32.350
an island, where Santa and his reindeer find
themselves in some dangerous situations.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:37.889
"I'm just missing Prancer and Rudolph."

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:44.100
In '''Tis the Fifteenth season," Homer demonstrates
ample selfishness by buying an expensive gizmo

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.660
for himself, severely limiting the rest of
the family's Christmas budget.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:52.220
After watching a Mr. Magoo-inspired cartoon
that mimics A Christmas Carol, Homer decides

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.770
to be the most charitable person in town,
instead of a selfish oaf.

00:04:55.770 --> 00:05:00.250
This development enrages Ned Flanders, because
being charitable is his trademark.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.150
Later in the episode, Homer pulls a "Grinch
who stole Christmas" because Lisa tells him

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.910
everyone would be happier if society was a
little less materialistic.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:12.530
As a whole, "'Tis the Fifteenth Season" demonstrates
how television writers can copy and paste

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:17.570
pre-existing pop culture and explain that
their intent was satire or homage, as opposed

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.160
to exploring new ideas.

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.750
When Homer tells his family about the cartoon
he watched, Lisa explains to him that the

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.290
storyline is nothing new.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.550
"Dad, what you saw was a Christmas Carol.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.600
It was written by Charles Dickens 160 years
ago."

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.890
"Yeah, TV writers have been milking that goat
for years."

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:37.260
After Mr. Burns pays for repairs at Reverend
Lovejoy's church during season thirteen's

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:41.270
"She of Little Faith," the heartless miser
insists that the place of worship be covered

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.660
in advertising.

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.490
Upset by the feckless capitalism and trivialized
spirituality, Lisa converts to Buddhism with

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.810
a little help from guest star Richard Gere.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:53.780
Once again, The Simpsons stumbles into the
paradox that has sunk other episodes: It wants

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:58.250
to use religion for comedic purposes but runs
up against an imperative to never suggest

00:05:58.250 --> 00:06:00.190
that mainstream religion is funny.

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:02.290
Luckily, this episode doesn't feel like a
cop-out.

00:06:02.290 --> 00:06:07.790
"Ohhh, you'll get yours."

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:12.580
During the story's second half, Marge's campaign
to lure Lisa back into the flock gets so manipulative

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.360
that even Homer gives her an incredulous glance,
making this a rare Simpsons episode in which

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.030
Homer plays the voice of reason.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:24.840
In season eleven's "Grift of the Magi ," "Kid
First Industries" bails out Springfield Elementary,

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.270
and much like the narrative of "She of Little
Faith," commercialization runs totally amok.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:33.389
Quite unlike "She of Little Faith," this episode
features a celebrity cameo from Gary Coleman

00:06:33.389 --> 00:06:37.669
that feels inspired and purposeful, in contrast
to Richard Gere's walk-on.

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:42.460
It's hard to say how many members of the television-viewing
public under 40 years old have ever seen Different

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:43.460
Strokes.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.840
But even if younger fans don't recognize Coleman,
plenty of viewers know that he delivered one

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.080
of the best Simpsons cameos ever.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.400
"I'm sorry Gary, there's no longer a place
for you here."

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.240
"Whatchu you talkin' about, Miss Neagle?"

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.199
"That is so adorable.

00:06:57.199 --> 00:06:58.710
You're rehired."

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:03.020
"The Way of the Dog" is a 2020 episode that
finds Santa's Little Helper losing control

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:07.790
of his unresolved emotional issues, which
leads to him getting a little bitey with Marge.

00:07:07.790 --> 00:07:11.919
A dog behaviorist voiced by Cate Blanchett
figures out that his problems are related

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:13.830
to trauma from when he was a puppy.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.780
So, the Simpsons track down a dog trainer
who proudly admits to separating their pooch

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:18.780
from his mother.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:22.070
Of course, the family has a perfectly natural
reaction to this news.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:23.190
(Slap) "Ow."

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:24.190
(Slap.

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.190
Slap.)

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:26.540
"Here, Maggie" (Slap) "Ow."

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:30.740
The episode ends with the Simpsons bringing
home Santa's Little Helper's mother, the perfectly

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:31.830
named She Biscuit.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:37.100
Bart accidentally melts the family's plastic
Christmas tree and destroys all of their presents

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.510
in the episode "Miracle on Evergreen Terrace."

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:43.100
Being Bart, he blames the whole fiasco on
an imaginary burglar.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:47.060
After a news report airs about the supposed
break-in, the residents of Springfield rally

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:49.639
together to give the Simpsons a merry Christmas.

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:53.900
When the truth eventually comes out, there's
an understandable backlash against the family.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:58.770
The final few minutes, in which the town righteously
loots 742 Evergreen Terrace, are easily the

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.070
funniest few of the entire episode.

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:10.880
"If you're heading for the medicine cabinet,
I already been there."

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.699
In "Skinner's Sense of Snow," a blizzard traps
Bart, Lisa, and plenty of other students at

00:08:14.699 --> 00:08:18.169
Springfield Elementary during the final day
before winter break.

00:08:18.169 --> 00:08:21.890
Most of the jokes in the episode revolve around
Principal Skinner's futile attempt to maintain

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:22.890
order.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:26.460
Eventually, Skinner starts to lose his cool
and takes his frustrations out on Groundskeeper

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:27.460
Willie.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:30.509
That's the last time you'll slap your Willie
around.

00:08:30.509 --> 00:08:32.200
I quit."

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:36.959
There are also a few apparent lapses of in-story
logic here, and not necessarily the kind we

00:08:36.959 --> 00:08:39.319
can wave off just because it's a cartoon.

00:08:39.319 --> 00:08:44.089
Lisa revels in anti-establishment chaos in
a manner that clashes with the highly respectful

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:47.480
attitude toward the education system she usually
displays.

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:51.350
Plus, Skinner spends much of the episode's
second half stuck in a dodgeball bag that

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:54.709
doesn't seem like it should be able to bind
an adult human.

00:08:54.709 --> 00:08:59.459
Speaking of Skinner's adulthood, this episode
aired in 2000 and states his age as 40.

00:08:59.459 --> 00:09:03.689
But in a flashback, it also depicts him as
an adult soldier during the Vietnam War.

00:09:04.709 --> 00:09:08.720
For many fans, some of the most memorable
Simpsons episodes and segments take place

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:09.720
in the future.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.720
It's always fun to see how much Lisa has achieved
and how little Bart has changed.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:17.949
The 2011 episode "Holidays of Future Passed"
is no exception.

00:09:17.949 --> 00:09:21.189
Thirty years in the future, the Simpsons reunite
to spend Christmas together.

00:09:21.189 --> 00:09:25.920
Along the way, we learn that Lisa is a successful
business woman and, with her husband Milhouse,

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.499
has an angsty teenage daughter.

00:09:27.499 --> 00:09:30.579
Bart, of course, is a down-and-out divorced
dad.

00:09:30.579 --> 00:09:34.440
But the big surprise here is that Maggie is
a world-famous rock star and she recently

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.209
gave birth to her first child.

00:09:36.209 --> 00:09:41.399
The story also features ungrateful Simpson
grandkids, dozens of adult Ralph Wiggum clones,

00:09:41.399 --> 00:09:45.189
and an unusually optimistic vision of Homer
as an old man.

00:09:45.189 --> 00:09:48.660
But the emotional highlight of the episode
occurs when Bart and Lisa have a few too many

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:53.740
drinks in their old treehouse and Bart makes
a confession to his overachieving sister.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.459
"You're the person I always wanted to be."

00:09:57.459 --> 00:09:58.459
"Awww…" (hugs)

00:10:00.929 --> 00:10:04.819
The series premiere of the longest-running
scripted television show in the history of

00:10:04.819 --> 00:10:10.139
the medium almost feels too historic to be
contextualized as one of several holiday-specific

00:10:10.139 --> 00:10:11.139
Simpsons episodes.

00:10:11.139 --> 00:10:15.220
But then again, it is very much a Simpsons
holiday special, even if it might also be

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.860
other things of equal or greater significance.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:22.350
It took The Simpsons until roughly eight episodes
into its second season to really figure itself

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:26.360
out and rise to the immaculate level we associate
with the show.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.860
But "Simpsons Roasting on an Open Fire" certainly
contains hints of the absurdity, irreverent

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:34.490
self-awareness, and heart that was coming
down the pipeline.

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:36.329
Maybe "hints" is putting it too lightly.

00:10:36.329 --> 00:10:38.970
Homer's stint as a mall Santa is very absurd.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.209
Bart's dialogue is already very irreverent
and self-aware.

00:10:42.209 --> 00:10:45.519
And the family's decision to give Santa's
Little Helper a forever home, even though

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:49.920
he failed to win them a dime at the race track,
checks off the heart box, for sure.

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:55.199
Ironically, the top-ranking Simpsons holiday
episode according to fans isn't really about

00:10:55.199 --> 00:10:56.199
Christmas.

00:10:56.199 --> 00:11:00.059
To put it more precisely, "Marge Be Not Proud,"
isn't addressing boilerplate, generalized

00:11:00.059 --> 00:11:02.110
themes about family or togetherness.

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:06.300
Instead, it's the definitive Bart episode,
with Christmas functioning as a plot device.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:10.959
In the story, the eldest Simpson child gets
busted shoplifting a video game, and Marge

00:11:10.959 --> 00:11:13.519
responds as if it's the worst thing he's ever
done.

00:11:13.519 --> 00:11:17.720
Sure, Bart caused an international incident
as recently as the previous season, but in

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:22.569
terms of the kind of non-fantastical mischief
that a ten-year-old boy could commit in real

00:11:22.569 --> 00:11:26.100
life, ripping off the Try-N-Save is his greatest
transgression.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:30.600
Bart puts actual effort into reestablishing
the doting mother-son dynamic he rejects at

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:34.000
the beginning of the episode when he claims
that he's not a little kid anymore.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.800
While he's happy to play the part of his generation's
Dennis the Menace, he doesn't want his mayhem

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.220
to go too far and alienate his family.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:41.970
Or, for that matter, his audience.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:47.040
"Marge Be Not Proud" established a rule The
Simpsons has more or less followed since:

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.399
Bart's shenanigans must remain either too
cartoony for reality or too harmless for anyone

00:11:51.399 --> 00:11:52.699
to take seriously.

00:11:52.699 --> 00:11:57.259
His character walks that fine line and usually
caps it off with one of his many catchphrases.

00:11:57.259 --> 00:12:00.139
"Eat my shorts."

